\begin_layout Title
Macreconomic Theory I: Homework 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An economy consists of two infinitely lived consumers named 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 There is one nonstorable consumption good.
 Consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 consumes 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 ranks consumption streams by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(c_{t}^{i})}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)$
\end_inset

 is increasing, strictly concave, and twice continuously differentiable.
 Consumer 1 is endowed with a stream of the consumption good 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{1}=\left(1,0,0,1,0,0,1,\ldots\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Consumer 2 is endowed with a stream of the consumption good 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{2}=\left(0,1,1,0,1,1,0,\ldots\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Assme that there are complete markets with time-0 trading.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Define a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
competitive equilibrium 
\emph default
is a feasible allocation and a price system that, given the price system,
 the allocation solves each household's problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Compute a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a special case in which 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 is deterministic and alternates between the values of 1 and 0.
 Thus, the endowment processes are perfectly predictable sequences.
 Note that the aggregate endowment is constant, 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}y_{t}^{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{1}\ \mbox{and}\ c_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

 are constant over time.
 Thus, the equilibrium allocation satifies 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}=\bar{c}^{i}\ \forall\ t,\ \mbox{for}\ i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's problem is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
 & {\displaystyle \max_{c_{t}^{i}}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(\bar{c}^{i})}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}q_{t}^{0}\bar{c}^{i}\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}q_{t}^{0}y_{t}^{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}$
\end_inset

 is the price of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 consumption in terms of an abstract unit of account or numeraire.
 So, we shall set 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}^{0}=1$
\end_inset

, putting the price system in units of time 0 goods.
 Underlying the single budget constraint is the fact that multilateral trades
 are possible through a clearing operation that keeps track of net claims.
 All trades occur at time 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

.
 After time 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

, trades that were agreed to at time 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 are executed, but no more trades occur.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both households have a single budget constraint to which we attach a Lagrange
 multiplier 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(\bar{c}^{i})}+\mu_{i}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}q_{t}^{0}y_{t}^{i}-{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}q_{t}^{0}\bar{c}^{i}\right]
\]

\end_inset

We obtain the first-order conditions for the household's problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\bar{c}^{i}} & = & \beta^{t}u^{\prime}(\bar{c}^{i})-\mu_{i}q_{t}^{0}=0\nonumber \\
 & \iff & q_{t}^{0}=\beta^{t}\frac{u^{\prime}(\bar{c}^{i})}{\mu_{i}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Equation 1 implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{\prime}(\bar{c}^{i})=\mu_{i}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ q_{0}^{0}=1)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{t}^{0}=\beta^{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation (2) and household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's budget constraint imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}[y_{t}^{i}-\bar{c}^{i}] & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Solving this equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{c}^{i}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{c}^{i}=(1-\beta){\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}y_{t}^{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{c}^{1}=(1-\beta){\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{3t}=}\frac{1-\beta}{1-\beta^{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{c}^{2}=1-\bar{c}^{1}=1-\frac{1-\beta}{1-\beta^{3}}=\frac{1-\beta^{3}}{1-\beta^{3}}-\frac{1-\beta}{1-\beta^{3}}=\frac{\beta(1-\beta^{2})}{1-\beta^{3}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c) Suppose that one of the consumers markets a derivative asset that promises
 to pay 
\begin_inset Formula $.05$
\end_inset

 units of consumption each period.
 What would the price of that asset be?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ d_{t}\right\} _{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 be a stream of claims on time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The price of an asset entitling the owner to this stream must be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{0}^{0}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}q_{t}^{0}d_{t}}
\]

\end_inset

If this equation did not hold, someone could make unbounded profits by synthesiz
ing this asset through purhcases or sales of dated commodities and then
 either buying or selling the asset.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\ \forall\ t\ q_{t}^{0}=\beta^{t}\ \mbox{and}\ d_{t}=0.5$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{0}^{0}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}0.05=\frac{1}{20(1-\beta)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

